  ('Receptionist', 'GB', "No, this course is held at our smaller site, the Ashcombe Building. That's A-S-H-C-O-M-B-E. We use it because it has a flat roof, where the tutor has set up a small weather station. Students take readings from the instruments during every session, so by the end you'll have two months of your own records. It's on Mill Lane, about five minutes' walk from the railway station."),
  ('Caller', 'AU', "That sounds great. And what does the course actually cover?"),
  ('Receptionist', 'GB', "In the first few weeks, you learn to identify the main types of cloud and what they tell you about the weather that's coming. Then you move on to air pressure and wind, and how to read the weather charts you see on television. In the final two weeks, everyone produces their own forecast for the local area, and then compares it with what actually happens. People usually find that part quite humbling."),
